{"seq":2,"at":"2026-10-20","kind":"edited","target":{"type":"article","id":"a0467"},"reason":"Rewritten by the merge to take in the printed cards' questions.","by":"merge everyday/food","ref":"quiz-archive/runs/tp-ingest/decisions/everyday__food.g3.json","changes":[{"field":"bodyEn","diff":[["=",626],["+","Provence, ratatouille shows what summer does with the same aubergine. The vegetable stew originated in Nice, probably as a purely local dish of the surrounding countryside, and its recipe was reportedly first printed only around 1930: aubergine, courgette, sweet peppers, onion and tomato, stewed slowly in olive oil with garlic and herbs. The name comes from the Occitan ratatolha, from a French verb meaning to stir.\n\nIn "],["=",284],["+","Ratatouille | Nice, Provence | Aubergine and courgette |\n| "],["=",32],["-","What"],["+","Ratatouille"],["=",1],["-","the"],["+","apart,"],["=",1],["-","five"],["+","what"],["=",1],["+","they "],["=",77]]},{"field":"bodyDe","diff":[["=",580],["+","der Provence zeigt die Ratatouille, was der Sommer aus derselben Aubergine macht. Der Gemüseeintopf stammt aus Nizza, wohl als rein lokales Gericht des Umlands, und sein Rezept wurde angeblich erst um 1930 gedruckt: Aubergine, Zucchini, Paprika, Zwiebeln und Tomaten, langsam in Olivenöl mit Knoblauch und Kräutern geschmort. Der Name kommt vom okzitanischen ratatolha, von einem französischen Verb für „umrühren“.\n\nIn "],["=",290],["+","Ratatouille | Nizza, Provence | Aubergine und Zucchini |\n| "],["=",28],["-","Was"],["+","Von"],["=",1],["-","die"],["+","der"],["=",1],["-","fünf"],["+","Ratatouille"],["=",1],["-","gemeinsam"],["+","abgesehen,"],["=",1],["-","haben,"],["+","haben"],["=",1],["-","ist"],["+","sie"],["=",9],["-","Lagerung:"],["+","Lagerung"],["=",1],["+","gemeinsam: "],["=",57]]}]},
